course: dictionary
lesson: 4279
title: Greed: wanting too much
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Greed means wanting much more of something than you need, or more than is fair. It can be money, food or power. The words around it are its context, and they tell you whether greed is good or bad in a story.
explain: «Virtues are good habits such as honesty and courage; vices are bad habits such as greed and cruelty.» Greed sits beside cruelty, so it is a bad habit. The word vices, which means bad habits, is the opposite of virtues, which means good habits.
explain: «Character thinking says we become what we repeatedly do: practise honesty and courage and you grow honest and brave; practise greed and cruelty and you grow greedy and cruel.» Greed is something you practise. The more you do it, the greedier you grow.
explain: «Sometimes greed goes unnoticed — nobody counts the pencils — but the person left with the broken ones still notices.» Greed can hide in small things, like taking more than your share. Check your meaning: does "taking too much" fit in that sentence? It does, so you have it right.
fact: Greed means wanting much more than you need, or more than is fair.
fact: In church teaching, greed (also called avarice) is one of the seven deadly sins.
fact: In stories, greed often leads to trouble: King Midas wanted everything to turn to gold, and the dog with the bone lost it by being greedy.
fact: In everyday life, greed means not sharing and taking more than your share.
fact: «Greed is good» is a famous line meant to be shocking, because most people see greed as a bad thing.
exercise: «Virtues are good habits such as honesty and courage; vices are bad habits such as greed and cruelty.» Is greed a good habit or a bad habit? | *Bad habit | A kind of courage | A type of honesty | Good habit
exercise: King Midas wanted everything to turn to gold. What kind of trouble does this story show? | Being brave in a storm | Sharing his gold with everyone | *Greed leading to trouble
exercise: «Greed for more and more things hides what you already have.» According to this sentence, what does greed hide? | *What you already have | The way home | The sky at night
test: What does greed mean? | Nothing at all | *Much more than you need, or more than is fair | A small bit of help for a friend | Less than you need
test: What does greed mean in everyday life? | Giving away your toys | *Not sharing, taking more than your share | Helping at the bus stop | Forgetting your homework
exam: In «practise greed and cruelty and you grow greedy and cruel», what does the word practise tell us about greed? | Greed is a kind of sport | Greed happens only once in a lifetime | *Greed is a habit you grow into by doing it again and again | Greed is something you are born with and cannot change
